WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.520
Tool calling and function calling allow an AI system to request capabilities from Python code or external services.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:18.820
A tool might read a record, search documents, create a ticket, send email, update a calendar, query a database, call an API, inspect code, or interact with an approved browser workflow.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.240
Tools are useful because they connect language to real work.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:28.220
They are risky because untrusted content may influence which tool is requested and with what arguments.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:32.280
Read-only tools and write-capable tools need different levels of control.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.860
Read-only tools can still leak sensitive information, especially when retrieval or identity checks are weak.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:47.040
Write-capable tools can change systems, send communications, approve access, modify data, trigger workflows, or affect external services.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:56.460
Least privilege, scoped credentials, allowlisted tools, allowlisted actions, rate limits, quotas, and audit trails keep tool access bounded.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.240
Argument validation is a critical guardrail.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:12.260
The application should validate resource identifiers, tenant context, destination addresses, message recipients, file names, requested operations, sizes, formats, and business intent before a tool executes.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.840
Safe defaults should prefer no action over ambiguous action.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:19.500
Dry-run behavior can show what would happen without making the change.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:25.640
Approval gates should be used for high-impact actions where a person or trusted policy must review the proposed operation.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.780
Untrusted content should not be able to trigger sensitive tools through the model.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.640
A retrieved document can suggest an action, but it should not authorize that action.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:39.420
A user can request a task, but the application must still verify permissions.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:46.520
An agent can plan, but the application should enforce boundaries, budgets, stop conditions, and review points.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:50.580
The safe pattern is model-assisted action under application control.
